#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a1c9f9-e81b-5cbf-b887-56485f0f137d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(y-250),\\,y(0)=200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241190} {:answer/id #uuid "12bbdcf7-e5f8-5520-a549-ec4ef787a73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241341} {:answer/id #uuid "26fad6ae-6f30-5c85-b6c8-fde919f0e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241514} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad2cc2d-67d7-52ae-bcdd-b3b3e4ed9748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y(y-250),\\,y(0)=50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241853} {:answer/id #uuid "8a30250b-009d-56da-9a4e-ca03739d84e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-3(y+250),\\,y(0)=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241514} :answer-field/id #uuid "177efa5e-2726-5c7f-b592-713face07fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f97edbb-38b9-5a3e-9db9-d7a29ce3a4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-106663" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=3,a=250,$ and ${y}_{0}=150.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=400>a=250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b74483-29bf-585d-92c8-7ccb3f928a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(70-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445333} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445348} {:answer/id #uuid "993db06a-cd74-5472-8bca-5491bd9aed54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(70-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445353} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf94966-8385-545a-b21e-358160ff193e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445371} {:answer/id #uuid "f700ef32-7ece-558e-a620-e91790aaa0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445348} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06da1af-5ad6-5a97-9279-9b6036bd2a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106691" :question/problem "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $\\text{units/day}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=70\\text{ units/day},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ab2b17-4102-57ca-9ab3-3ab110b225b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272469} {:answer/id #uuid "4ffdb4a5-548d-535b-93a7-3bfc67a56a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$21$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273062} {:answer/id #uuid "6023a2e0-53e0-5e0b-aabb-dd01395c05f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$17$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273215} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf7ce8a-3d39-5462-bbe2-3ed9759ce56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273317} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c0f40-2268-54a8-b736-b8c947a033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$45$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273317} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d9f12c-be81-5b55-b153-dbf17a4401ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "754ac309-f23b-53d4-8c3a-d4c7dd3dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15370" :question/problem "A University Campus has a total of $1\\,500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the campus population affected by the flu, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total campus population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total population of the campus, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,500$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial campus population affected by the flu was $300$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=300,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K{e}^{0} & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K & =300 \\\\ K & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the campus population affected by flu was $500$ students. So, applying the condition $P(10)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-10k} & =500 \\\\ 1\\,000 & =1\\,200{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $800$ students are affected by the flu. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =800 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =800 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $800$ students will be affected by the flu after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}=29.562…≈30\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d14aa6-7bfa-5d51-a2d3-b51157905d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(105 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122997} {:answer/id #uuid "8deef86d-850d-580f-936a-7b20c647e281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(1700 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122998} {:answer/id #uuid "034daa26-4afc-57bc-9d72-1a947446d502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(65 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122999} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9240f3-5806-5595-840a-e234d5f16554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(55 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123000} {:answer/id #uuid "4f16b14f-06bc-5798-ad2e-b30a874d50ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(85 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123001}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be720ede-1dbc-51a2-b8eb-db9c18720fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122996}] :question/id #uuid "bdadb029-2e6a-54b7-8c42-d0b37b92a134" :question/math-academy-id "q-37376" :question/problem "A rehabilitation center initially has $20$ children. The number of children in the center $R(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the center is $85$ children, which differential equation could be used to model the population of the rehabilitation center? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a1b567-4d92-50bc-9b67-c3e259e0a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$468$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123009} {:answer/id #uuid "e5843acc-3b0b-5b91-aef2-abf1ba9f4627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$489$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123010} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3ffd56-67f0-5967-8b78-0029b0163067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$420$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123011} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5bb5bc-7e52-5d0e-a372-536b69460728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$486$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123012} {:answer/id #uuid "690e1fd9-4928-5f3a-bdee-05eccf3128d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$442$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186123009} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0d7ef1-07b3-513a-a823-292131ee0d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3ce256b3-4ef6-51c4-ab72-48603b95ab11" :question/math-academy-id "q-37389" :question/problem "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6,$ respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42?$\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the egg production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible egg production and its current egg production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ eggs, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(500-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{500-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{500-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the egg production was $390$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(3)=390,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(3) & =390 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =390 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $6,$ the egg production was $400$ eggs. So, applying the condition $E(6)=400,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =400 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-6k} & =400 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-6k}} & =\\frac{110}{100} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{11}{10} \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{11}{10}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{11}{10}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} & =110 \\\\ K & =110{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)} \\\\ K & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=500-121{e}^{-(t/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of eggs laid on day $t=42$ is\n\n$$\nE(42)=500-121{e}^{-(42/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11/10)}≈468\\,\\text{eggs},\n$$\n\n rounded to nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0160e30a-5846-5aa6-86ef-a585cedd44ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$986$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321161} {:answer/id #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} {:answer/id #uuid "78c60709-1e3a-58f3-8b53-ecf0dc4c70cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1005$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322096} {:answer/id #uuid "cd55f1ff-b05d-5c57-bf3a-581ad4b6e03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$942$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322746} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf858e4-c93a-5010-9a90-68721ca8ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$850$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bfea20-78cb-5933-b3c2-3d3bdf908386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" :question/math-academy-id "q-37400" :question/problem "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151905 :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\\left|\n\t\t{\\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array}}\n\t\\right|\t\n= y_1 y_2'-  y_2 y_1'.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151906 :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681" :question/problem "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\t\t{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right|}\t\n= y_1y_2'- y_2 y_1'.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151907 :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151908 :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683" :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} &=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5433a9-72db-51ce-937e-66eb03873dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305766} {:answer/id #uuid "8b917115-33ec-5d50-a88d-2352497eac30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306480} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2be0f4-c850-5f8b-b2ae-bd634c13d2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306631} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f87aa4-ec37-5a9f-ae7c-64639217a5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306480} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db6c097-e3e8-592b-949a-c77ecf3fd71f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3774c6bc-bcfb-5d9c-8f60-cd1fab8d5abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305684} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a939ab-a1ca-58b7-998c-54770b1d8482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305684} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b0cc90-db84-561b-97c8-0f2811a22120" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5de205-a939-5790-bf0f-80dec1102338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306348} {:answer/id #uuid "82ef74d2-7ac2-5367-9bba-6875918074da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306402} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e5eab2-435b-53d1-a757-4ead9cca4d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306754} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2204f-098e-50ce-aa7a-6a579c439319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8d50d6-5f39-5ea9-acec-5edc0397d212" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163e3e74-406d-5c87-badb-df3fe7f86f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305346} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc4d041-b76d-50e9-8227-69f16d8bfeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7643f3f8-9c47-557d-a64f-afef74d62cb0" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063714e5-6877-54ec-a14a-8e57cf35f257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305209} {:answer/id #uuid "3144887e-20f3-5944-9672-87543320b1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305616} {:answer/id #uuid "52a698c9-78c0-5a0b-b51a-419d9e0dd7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305950} :answer-field/id #uuid "f366e359-c82a-5d61-8e0a-af3bc724c8f3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32cf0cf3-3509-510a-a72a-b72974c6e70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333508" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,2x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f463b29-3d8f-5cdf-83a4-bf6229b51dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref