_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf $x = 0$, we get {{field-1}}.\n\nIf $x = \\frac{π}{6}$, we get {{field-2}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b1bcd6-0062-5e8c-8449-431d5deb4587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149850} {:answer/id #uuid "faf26824-bf9d-5443-a298-9bd78569651a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149851}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3ade48-354c-5bfc-96eb-4b96c9a3db46" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab08f566-e03d-5418-8519-9506ee180280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149853} {:answer/id #uuid "413f67a6-47a5-5962-aed0-e064077eacd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149854}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0576399b-7b83-5613-b417-3c9e6457a93b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11849504-966c-58fa-863a-9d925407d3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391202} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2a069c-609e-583c-978a-6c288d9af47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391202} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b272f6-637b-5ea0-8700-e5f7d86f7f6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512dbb1c-ad34-53bb-b6aa-d476941e80d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391205} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed2bc6-a9a6-5220-82fe-401940c11d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391205} :answer-field/id #uuid "42fb6754-3f60-584f-8e8c-64f87a1b9091" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391204} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848ba988-ee07-5611-b978-c788c6c35a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391208} {:answer/id #uuid "99d0bffe-aea4-5f88-a834-76108f8dae94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391209} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cbdd8d-c657-558a-b8d1-c64ef71723c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391210} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca3db64-7ada-5db5-b594-508ba910d0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391210} :answer-field/id #uuid "a961eff4-a4cc-5f40-8e2c-1194d07931d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ccce0b3-5f04-5d5a-9e2d-ebcf2aa205bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333517" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87780ffc-068d-529d-b3ae-4de8a04ef6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149861} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcea6b5-b568-52aa-9196-7b8010a3958c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149862} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0c7657-946f-5aca-a551-8f052966c191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\cos^{2} (2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149863} {:answer/id #uuid "2019699f-7d85-5794-b862-e30e190c25ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sin (4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149864}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ae08e4-9935-57f7-b5bb-035a52a188ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5d0dc6b-6eca-5b5c-9694-5e5ee6c0b8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149866} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe07769-0bd7-5427-984b-eeedeca535a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149867}] :answer-field/id #uuid "218fbae4-f859-546d-ac61-1f2a42de8fe0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c643685b-6989-585f-aa86-85cbaf87c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149869} {:answer/id #uuid "21d582b7-4ef3-5991-99ed-4392241acaa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149870}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5d4e20-83ea-58f4-9c45-85ea2720ba6a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149868}] :question/id #uuid "04d27662-e8db-50e4-b0fb-c1f9b36a368e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333518" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}(x) = \\cos (2x), y_{2}(x) = \\sin (2x), x ∈ R\n$$\nConsider the functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ above. Given that $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(y_{1}, y_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01457e13-545a-541f-acce-7e047fbcc408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459799} {:answer/id #uuid "87fffe77-2143-5ff9-a2da-d1695227d7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459800} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5a869e-488e-5c2d-b679-699f90c7fb17" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c86362a-34b4-52d6-ad75-a83bcdd09134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459801} {:answer/id #uuid "f98b348b-31e2-5430-8135-c980ff086ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459802} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd47a839-63c4-5a6d-b3f2-177b46628555" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d15df765-6a00-5bd1-8191-5dfbfb9a0320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459803} {:answer/id #uuid "41ca8881-af27-5681-851a-1d131d4cd84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2930a8aa-b164-5efa-ac05-ac74a56ddd9f" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0983dd-268a-5729-9ff0-7442930a90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668012} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f2f4f8-943e-5891-84ce-151c6163fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668012} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f62d2b-8b37-5011-9317-8232e0614af8" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5244118-e12b-5ac2-ae1e-3e2ab9060947" :question/math-academy-id "q-333520" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e82711-ac51-5228-a804-1fa990c75224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459805} {:answer/id #uuid "863f65d1-6c09-5fc7-9229-f2192ef8b701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459806} :answer-field/id #uuid "782a33f2-c589-5a92-89c3-730260ecc10a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d25a391a-473d-59a2-a939-6f566cc09ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459807} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ed9d3b-064f-5134-a779-fd16eac2b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459807} :answer-field/id #uuid "48062bbd-ee12-5738-a47f-04a9032c16f2" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a6bdefd-4eb4-5101-b957-85e5319b7fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459809} {:answer/id #uuid "92b100bd-b5bc-5ebe-a3ef-e3746c7a2aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459809} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03c3346-2011-57c2-9e66-8f81c201e4aa" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a59468-f654-511b-b7a1-04f01a614ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668014} {:answer/id #uuid "2584cc39-9a67-5af0-9c26-e66ed00ead44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668014} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca4921a-1f2e-5a99-8549-861873b044c0" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e7ec306-a94e-573b-92ac-0141248c8937" :question/math-academy-id "q-333522" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec064a4d-7102-57fc-b186-402daf8b164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} {:answer/id #uuid "189c918e-7d05-5308-8e1d-edf7c3216627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150170} {:answer/id #uuid "4c66de07-d217-52ea-a332-34af866e7b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150171} {:answer/id #uuid "53649d44-816e-5797-bda4-28adc599b7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421262} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5866a6-934d-5826-8186-efe3dbb229ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8068aa-dbbd-5b69-9937-8b4236d86198" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd30eee5-3768-5759-bb64-72e553a332ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-64710" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "946017a0-ae4d-5540-a4b4-27053d50db7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150174} {:answer/id #uuid "94b189b2-5329-5305-a660-ed9eea5ce286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150175} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78fa17-4129-5d8b-9b85-c2a11a6f03b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150176} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f11604-fca5-56a9-8672-4d58f5562cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150177} {:answer/id #uuid "3a079125-cccc-5599-a6f0-b5c0b4f41bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150178}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0204fa-3031-5534-9990-4f98e2f004e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150173}] :question/id #uuid "49b9e009-4dbc-5320-96ca-20137aa6f00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64745" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_{1}, y_{2})$ for $y_{1}(x) = x^{2} - 1$ and $y_{2}(x) = x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166533 :question/id #uuid "86054cff-8fae-561d-a57e-31e573a45a91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21585" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{x}(0) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{3t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} + 2e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166534 :question/id #uuid "63995ae4-21fa-5777-85ca-93585258b9f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21586" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/66/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166535 :question/id #uuid "e0888bda-120f-52bb-a6f9-c5af9737139f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21588" :question/problem "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](images/0c/0cf7942e618f0465ead5b98c6404a3747e505db75517725971c833c97121ab64.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](images/10/105bdbdcac5f0ff20f71a0131bf5bb3cbafe0e93ce8bc733a51c2dcb3fb8364f.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](images/96/96757a3aee3b161b2ba9887cd85f977f063807779604647dd791cf265790cabc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](images/65/658c15d2291368d0e6836ba19b90157d49dee4a510d58cddf7f85ddc9ff72a8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6dbc83-a1e5-5183-ba0e-87fb31dcfaf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d5/d5ce5f752e2701e9adcac4c56c3e3b0aee5ae1fe4a4c4aeed8fe5151643ba96d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164164} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5a9dd2-62d2-5610-8058-02cd43261271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c7/c790ead248efa8a57e91525b0d0ff634359eb2101c6c0293922bf2d7d6cbc5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164165} {:answer/id #uuid "59a01ce3-1d48-51e4-892b-d6e21a53a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/67/67273ae9c0f9d50f1a95da3c61039fa9cf262a7b34b93cc9e6f1fe26cd463a5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164166} {:answer/id #uuid "76a810b4-fff3-5969-a6f9-048c8cc5ee41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9c/9c5881a3040105716e2735cc53b09f3ecfe5a1e812adae1f0870ddb8f6da6969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164167} {:answer/id #uuid "7752e51e-d8bf-56d4-86ee-658e1dc0896b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/11/115d62eba18cf3eff05d470049170e17776b204cb494afdfaf91022efe1c389d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164168}] :answer-field/id #uuid "575b4fd8-b9d4-577b-bc22-063f32c9d4b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164163}] :question/id #uuid "d6be91f3-66c4-570e-944c-46b93eb6fb0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-331177" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56363bc94f834c2a6d0b60a5864f33c69a0c7f69015ff4ef0c40394d6021629.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521d9b1e-2cce-5d2b-a31d-9cdb7b8ef1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302964} {:answer/id #uuid "63e3860b-7a26-5360-ae19-df9137a0d2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303060} {:answer/id #uuid "b5967db0-ab1f-5c45-a482-fdc287d27403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303579} {:answer/id #uuid "c46a5b5e-a90e-5b06-b94c-f22582ce6432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303664} {:answer/id #uuid "e86d7fb1-b5a2-57e4-9891-6bfd38d19893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303901} :answer-field/id #uuid "26290a84-1e00-5d8e-89b4-572bb2e8741b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa27b9e4-8a19-5583-914f-f8357a95c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331275" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x-2y \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x-3y \\\\  & =2(1)-3(-2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5d3b9e-fbc4-5a2f-95c2-20d87aed98c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302551} {:answer/id #uuid "a13cecbe-021d-59f1-898b-56cbea4639b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303451} {:answer/id #uuid "a237d52c-f368-59eb-ab3d-c9c554d09270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{29}{2} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303455} {:answer/id #uuid "a44412a6-9bf7-50d6-b540-08ed14cdb37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303466} {:answer/id #uuid "cdce8456-0f1b-5518-b299-6376125d6f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ \\frac{37}{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303466} :answer-field/id #uuid "320f5044-ab8c-5be0-a680-489f0e871c58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923e8ea5-006a-5c4e-a94f-6a9b7c029dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-331276" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(t)={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d3d593-5d00-5de8-bf14-024bdae09a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164188} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ea43de-b2d5-53bd-a777-23c39d5e8510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164189} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1bcf3d-c60e-552d-b102-1bedd29809f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164190} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb66478-cb47-5059-9571-3f19991ac349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164191}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f2747ca-0cbc-5816-ba92-1c8296bc5a91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493bbf94-36f1-59ba-b77d-add5e858e08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164193} {:answer/id #uuid "62a2b78b-594e-5182-8476-e2a000029918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164194} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4a6a5e-2249-500a-93b8-7970247e5c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164195} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0d19a7-df29-57eb-b964-9b173d76e8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164196}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7a34920e-5fac-5eb9-be07-5761d3174a5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c443583c-7e54-5f59-9ab1-89aee41bd3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390898} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4372c8-4a7c-57de-bb0e-0d0c81f647d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390899} {:answer/id #uuid "6f050c47-2efc-56c6-8db5-55a4a6a7be6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390900} {:answer/id #uuid "0af23e83-bdec-5481-933e-04324f2a459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390899} :answer-field/id #uuid "db6adcc9-e832-5c5c-a3b9-b8a6c52643f8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b432c2-0e0d-5009-8470-4e3571054d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390903} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d6625e-4e34-5b96-9274-5a9d7dfd284f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390904} {:answer/id #uuid "18035581-6355-53bd-aaff-403fe9c0130e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390905} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02896b-984a-534e-9478-eb60968c1e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390904} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3c9fa7-0d6e-5df1-9ab3-f0035bf250e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c96b74a-d34e-5ce3-b63d-86dac5a4ffc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-331282" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6ba5066fd4ddc43491c3d007355e0b64c84d449d6462812aa4c8ca0450887a8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b90f53-d0fd-5b27-abe2-e1dbe0de43f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164340} {:answer/id #uuid "f099e6e2-62f8-50ce-9374-b8b56fde38ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164341} {:answer/id #uuid "0d302b13-ef52-5c3f-ab6c-c765cc9ea540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164342} {:answer/id #uuid "58a820fe-0a82-5709-817f-a5b597f4e253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164343}] :answer-field/id #uuid "75df341c-02e1-5b72-9781-3e8e3197af8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73f5106-ea50-5c95-b4e0-ba04c87b1cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164345} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7a2649-93d7-5281-b96a-6eddcb6985a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164346} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed04dab-4691-58c0-96cf-b5432535d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164347} {:answer/id #uuid "41a7093f-fe23-504d-9325-0ec565355fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164348}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d3621bc5-62de-54a8-a7ec-90e03d5f1d81" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164344}] :question/id #uuid "0b943e2b-d0a4-5a84-b4f5-ee38b1ddf3c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332632" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - x ( t ) + 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-2}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9604b112-9c0b-5661-99bc-a927725a3aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/5852fdce770beeaa73192b4487c445a5b6cdd857a01b08465c60c1ffeeba9aed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164428} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43db8f-eb72-5379-ad90-7444b20614bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/df/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164429} {:answer/id #uuid "0028624f-4d5e-54f6-adb4-21cf50012c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6d/6daec64ca18c09907c43f10f5f2e1b46f693782bddcccb8fccd0240534120100.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164430} {:answer/id #uuid "7604128f-2e78-5d31-a4e5-14d621b093e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9f32c0e475c4973fed376a2392bad1b159123da933c6defb7145cc6ab6ce2634.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164431} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6f23f6-3aee-588d-a3b5-a87eb757b9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e265cd75cfd7cd4178a6d222d86043d97c816d36286b516898d8ff0b0e6d208c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164432}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e9489f-6e99-5682-ae80-2e8eb0cd2e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc017f0-fa0b-53d8-b90f-a0d020af9d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e265cd75cfd7cd4178a6d222d86043d97c816d36286b516898d8ff0b0e6d208c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391132} {:answer/id #uuid "266d074e-ce24-5bf4-988e-95150096a2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/5852fdce770beeaa73192b4487c445a5b6cdd857a01b08465c60c1ffeeba9aed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391133} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ca23fb-815f-5016-8412-6468504a8837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6d/6daec64ca18c09907c43f10f5f2e1b46f693782bddcccb8fccd0240534120100.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391134} {:answer/id #uuid "0662e1c8-a634-569b-ae58-c7f5dccb33ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/df/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391135} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cbcbee-4126-5a07-ad49-6ed0503afbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9f32c0e475c4973fed376a2392bad1b159123da933c6defb7145cc6ab6ce2634.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391135} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c010e65-847f-51f1-966d-9f1ee42eecc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c356d0ae-4461-596b-a446-f5773fdb52cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-332920" :question/problem "![](images/14/14ed16cc149ae777e195aa59e39cf809e939032a925e7acbd88cd685af99dd0e.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (straight to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/0d/0d010be8bad72bf4ebac4dbe2d325f1a1f194d78c676e2a62306b5c7d2af3c66.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right  but then starts turning downward.\n\n![](images/51/5118424696b9602f10bf116c3a292e2fb58ed2f9acd2a9507888ab92a9b8986b.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to move rightward and bend downward, converging to the origin.\n\n![](images/92/92a58573efafd7155cf02faf52e27ab968d9161ad448530b45673fd83a61fde1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233098 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f95fdc58-1eb7-5580-ab34-aae2969c5c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-107420" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{1-3x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{1-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-3{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{1-3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233099 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b5b538e-387a-5bc3-9ad6-828140780508" :question/math-academy-id "q-107458" :question/problem "If $f(x)={2}^{3-{x}^{3}},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={2}^{u},\\,u=3-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{3-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233778 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf1d61d4-5576-5207-ad64-f8866ebc8219" :question/math-academy-id "q-31261" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({e}^{x}+{8}^{x}+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({e}^{x}+{8}^{x}+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}8}+3\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
